  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\nWe'll use a hybrid optimization strategy that combines deterministic unimodal sequences with stochastic self-mutual constraints. The central idea is to first generate a base prototype from known minimal-convolution sequences, then refine it through multiple clustering-based stages with adaptive constraints. \n\nThis approach carefully balances the need for computational efficiency with the mathematical proof goals of minimizing the objective function. Unlike the LP-based previous method, which might get stuck in local optima due to its linear projection nature, our method uses a structured multiple-league refinement process.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\n- Input: parent sequence (length 768) if available, otherwise all-ones sequence\n- Output: sequence of length N (128 <= N <= 4096)\n- Internal representation: \n    - Maintain a canonical dense sequence (for primary optimization)\n    - Use sparse clustering representations (for fast search)\n    - Keep best-known solution across phases\n\nKey Formulation:\nLet P = max_k (a * a)[k]\nObjective: Minimize S = (2N * P) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n\nConstraints:\n- Non-negative coefficients: a_i \u2265 0\n- Finite support: 0 \u2264 i < N\n- Sum normalization: sum(a) \u2265 0.01\n- Smoothness factors: eCT \u2264 1.5 * (previous best)\n\nThe solver alternates between two phases:\n\nA. Prototype Greedy Refinement:\n    - Generate candidate sequences through spatial perturbation\n    - Maintain sum normalization\n    - Update best solution whenever S improves\n\nB. Adaptive Stochastic Strain:\n    - Uses self-mutation policy with momentum terms\n    - Projects gradients onto the coefficient space\n    - Gradually introduces controlled randomness to explore boundaries\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport cvxpy as cp\nfrom scipy.fft import rfft, irfft, fft, ifft\nfrom time import monotonic\nimport random\n\ndef propose_candidate(parent=None, budget=1800):\n    # 3.1 Initialization and setup\n    if parent is None:\n        parent = np.ones(512) / 512.0  # Fallback to uniform distribution\n        \n    n_base = len(parent)\n    # Ensure valid base sequence\n    s_base = np.sum(parent)\n    if s_base < 0.01:\n        return list(parent * s_base)\n    \n    best_sequence = parent[:]\n    best_score = float(evaluate_sequence(parent)) if np.isfinite(evaluate_sequence(parent)) else np.inf\n    \n    # 3.2 Generate amplifying perturbations with minimal computational cost\n    def mutate_base(a):\n        # Create shallow copies and mutations to avoid deep copying\n        a = np.array(a, copy=True)\n        # Momentum-based mutation\n        momentum = 0.8 * (np.random.random(a.shape) - 0.5)\n        # Limited perturbation amplitude\n        max_perturb = np.minimum(a * 0.2, 0.50)\n        perturb = np.random.normal(scale=0.1, size=a.shape) * max_perturb\n        # Saturate perturbations at boundaries\n        a = np.clip(a + perturb + momentum, 0.0, 1000.0)\n        return a / np.sum(a)\n    \n    # 3.3 Profit-driven tournaments between clusters\n    def tournament(a, b):\n        # Only compare if both are numerically valid\n        score_a = evaluate_sequence(a) if np.isfinite(sum(a)) else np.inf\n        score_b = evaluate_sequence(b) if np.isfinite(sum(b)) else np.inf\n        return a if score_a < score_b else b\n    \n    # 3.4 First-order prototyping with potential hashcat\n    def prototype_phase(a):\n        global best_sequence, best_score\n        \n        updates = []\n        # Generate clustered version by combining with self-mutants\n        while time_left > 600:  # Time guard\n            a_temp = np.random.normal(...)\n            if np.isfinite(evaluate_sequence(mutate(a_temp))):\n                updates.append(mutate_base(a_temp))\n                break if improve(updates[-1], best_score)\n                \n        # Linear combination refinement\n        score_vector = [evaluate_sequence(mutate_base(u)) for u in updates]\n        top_update = updates[np.argmin(score_vector)]\n        best_sequence = a.copy()\n        best_sequence = tolerate(best_sequence, best_score, top_update)\n        best_score = score_vector[0] if len(score_vector) > 0 else np.log10(time_left/100)\n        \n    # 3.5 Intrinsic refinements with adaptive constraints\n    def intrinsic_refine():\n        global best_sequence, best_score\n        new_n = choose_dimensions(len(best_sequence))\n        new_sequence = closest_uniform(new_n, best_sequence)\n        best_score = score_stable(tolerate, new_sequence)\n        best_sequence = new_sequence\n    \n    # 3.6 Main loop initialization\n    def main():\n        global best_sequence, best_score\n        time_start = monotonic()\n        time_tolerance = 1800 * 0.85\n        adaptive_clique_switch = time_leftwall\n        \n        # Perform multi-vector optimization\n        done = False\n        while not done and time_left > 0:\n            sub_sequence = any_vector(clique_s, best_sequence)\n            if sub_sequence is not None:\n                best_sequence = sub_sequence\n                best_score = optimize(sub_sequence)\n                done = threat_assurance()\n            else:\n                break\n        \n    # 3.7 Slot-based execution\n    class VectorWithSlots:\n        def __init__(self, vec):\n            self.vec = np.array(vec, copy=True) / sum(vec)\n            self.slot_vector = any_embedding(np.sort(vec)[::-1])\n        \n        def optimize(self):\n            return slot_filling(cvxpy_control, self)\n        \n        def improve(self):\n            slot_vectors = self.generate_clusters_from_slots()\n            # Spatial variations in amplitude across slots\n            enforced_amplitude = self.enforce_amplitude_budget()\n            refined_slot = optimal_slot_search(enforced_amplitude, slot_vectors)\n            return refined_slot\n    \n    # 3.8 Define all components as described in the strategy docs\n    